      "hints": [
        "Multiply by $(\\sin\\theta + 1)$ to get $\\cos\\theta + 4 + 5(\\sin^2\\theta - 1) = 0$.",
        "Replace $\\sin^2\\theta - 1 = -\\cos^2\\theta$ to show $5\\cos^2\\theta - \\cos\\theta - 4 = 0$."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Multiply the entire equation by the denominator $(\\sin\\theta + 1)$:\n$$\\cos\\theta + 4 + (5\\sin\\theta - 5)(\\sin\\theta + 1) = 0$$",
        "Expand the difference of two squares in the product:\n$$\\cos\\theta + 4 + 5(\\sin\\theta - 1)(\\sin\\theta + 1) = 0$$\n$$\\cos\\theta + 4 + 5(\\sin^2\\theta - 1) = 0$$",
        "Use the identity $\\sin^2\\theta - 1 = -\\cos^2\\theta$:\n$$\\cos\\theta + 4 + 5(-\\cos^2\\theta) = 0 \\implies \\cos\\theta + 4 - 5\\cos^2\\theta = 0$$\n$$5\\cos^2\\theta - \\cos\\theta - 4 = 0$$"
      ],
      "checking": {
        "mode": "ai",
        "ai_rubric": {
          "Trigonometric Proof": "Multiplies by $\\sin\\theta + 1$ (M1). Uses $\\sin^2\\theta = 1-\\cos^2\\theta$ (M1). Rearranges to $5\\cos^2\\theta - \\cos\\theta - 4 = 0$ (A1)."
        },
        "criteria_source": "mark_scheme_structured"
